      <h1>The North Hill Country</h1>
      <p>
        Where the Hill Country begins: limestone hills, live oak and shin oak,
        native grasses, and creeks and rivers running clear over rock. The
        northern tier offers the Hill Country look and lifestyle with more
        elbow room — and, in many areas, more working-land character — than
        the crowded southern counties.
      </p>

      <h2>The land</h2>
      <p>
        Elevation changes, hilltop views, and rocky soils that favor native
        pasture, wildlife habitat, and recreational use. Live water is the
        premium feature here — creek and river frontage tracts are the most
        sought-after land in the region, and priced like it.
      </p>

      <h2>Why buyers come here</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Recreation:</strong> hunting, fishing, and weekend-country living within reach of Austin and San Antonio.<br />
        <strong>Water:</strong> the Llano and San Saba river country and spring-fed creeks.<br />
        <strong>Views:</strong> genuine hilltop country for homesites and legacy ranches.<br />
        <strong>Towns:</strong> small towns with deep ranching roots — courthouse squares, feed stores, and locally owned cafés and shops.
      </p>

      <h2>Counties we know best here</h2>
      <p>San Saba, Llano, Mason, McCulloch, Burnet, Blanco, and Gillespie — see the <a href="/county-guides/">county guides</a> for detail by county.</p>

